            "showUrl": "/3D-Printable-Pet-Foot-Dispenser-With-Level-Sensing/step1/Hardware-Design/",
            "id": "S58B552M9MSUP12",
            "title": "Hardware Design",
            "body": "<p>The 3D-printable files (STL format) and the full assembly file (STEP format) are provided below. The RGB LED diffuser bar is printed from <a href=\"https://amzn.to/4io6sMr\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">white PLA+ filament<\/a>, with 2 x walls and no infill. The remainder of the parts can be printed using <a href=\"https://amzn.to/4lK3DZ9\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">black PLA+ filament<\/a>. If you'd like to print the parts yourself, you will need access to an FDM 3D printer with a sufficiently large build area (minimum 250 x 250 x 200mm), for example, this <a href=\"https://amzn.to/42JoanZ\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">open-source printer available on Amazon<\/a>. To print the parts, download the STL files and set up the print job using any free slicer software such as <a href=\"https://ultimaker.com/software/ultimaker-cura/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Cura<\/a>.<\/p><p>To assemble the enclosure, place the reservoir on top of the tray. The parts are meant to easily separate for adding food and maintaining the feeder, but if you'd like to hold any plastic parts held together more firmly, <a href=\"https://amzn.to/42JoanZ\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">black hot glue<\/a> with a <a href=\"https://amzn.to/4it8Bqb\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">hot glue gun<\/a> can be added.<\/p>",
            "url": "https://www.instructables.com/3D-Printable-Pet-Foot-Dispenser-With-Level-Sensing/step1/Hardware-Design/"
        },
        {
